== The answer to the question

The question was: once AI automates mental and physical labor entirely, what happens to our economy?

*It keeps producing, more than it ever has, and it stops distributing.* The machinery of production does not need wage earners in order to run, and it does not need them as customers either. Output goes to whoever owns the machines, the land, the energy and the legal rights that production still depends on, and they spend it on what they want and on building more. For everyone else the wage, which has been the way most people received their share for two hundred years, falls to the price of the machine that can do the same work, and that price is below the cost of living. The economy functions. It no longer functions for most people, unless they are given a claim on it that does not depend on their work.

I hold that conclusion with about 95 percent confidence, _given the premise_. It is closer to arithmetic than to prediction. The parts I hold less firmly are noted in @ch-wages: whether the machine price is below subsistence or merely far below today's wages, and how much paid work survives because people want it done by a person.

What happens next is not economics. Providing a comfortable income for every person would take a small share of what such an economy produces. Whether it is provided depends on whether the majority secured a durable claim while it still had leverage. A society that did would be the richest and freest in history. A society that did not would be one of abundance for owners, and a managed, dependent existence for everyone else, however cheap the goods.

== The next ten years: what I expect

I do not think the premise becomes fully true by 2036. I think it becomes true for most mental work around the end of this decade, is put into practice over the following ten years, and begins to become true for physical work as the decade ends. So the next ten years are the transition, and my central expectation for them is the scenario I called the squeeze. Here is that expectation in order, with the caveat that the dates are the softest part.

*2027.* Capability keeps climbing. Agents handle projects that take a person weeks, and using a computer the way a person does becomes reliable. Inside the AI companies, most engineering is done by machines. The revenue of the leading laboratories passes a quarter of a trillion dollars a year. Outside, the pattern of 2026 intensifies: firms hire few beginners into office work, the young unemployed and underemployed graduate becomes a fixture of the news, and average statistics stay calm. The financing of the build-out comes under strain, and some of the weaker builders of data centers fail. The new Congress holds hearings and passes little.

*2028 to 2029.* A recession, most likely beginning in this window. I do not know what sets it off; the financing of the build-out and high interest rates are the leading candidates. Firms use it to do what they had been putting off. Office staff are cut on a scale not seen before, and for the first time in an American recession the layoffs are heaviest among the educated and well paid. Unemployment reaches 7 or 8 percent. The 2028 election is fought largely on this. Both parties promise dividends from AI and both promise to protect jobs. Emergency checks go out. The government ends up holding shares in the AI companies, on terms that look generous to the companies.

*2030 to 2031.* The recovery arrives and the office jobs do not. Output grows 4 percent a year and employment barely moves. By now machines can do nearly any task that is done at a screen, and the argument shifts from whether to how fast. Congress passes the first structural measures: unemployment benefits that extend automatically, a tax on AI usage, a modest national dividend, public-service jobs, and a long list of roles in which the law requires a human. Skilled trades are in demand and well paid. Capable robots appear in warehouses and factories in the hundreds of thousands.

*2032 to 2033.* The Social Security trust fund runs short, sooner than once projected because payroll-tax receipts have sagged. The fix draws on the machine economy and sets a precedent. Driverless trucks and taxis become ordinary. The big office cities are in fiscal trouble: their tax bases were salaries and commercial property. A university degree no longer pays what it did, and enrollments fall.

*2034 to 2036.* Robots are produced by the million and their cost per hour drops below a few dollars. The physical refuge begins to close, in logistics and factories first. About three quarters of people aged 25 to 54 are in work. Labor's share of income is in the high forties. Machine-made goods and most services delivered through a screen are strikingly cheap; housing in desirable places, energy and anything that requires a licensed human are not. The wealthiest tenth own a larger share than they did. A national dividend exists and is small. The election of 2036 is about who owns the machines.

In 2036, on this path, the United States is not a post-labor economy. Machines do about a quarter of all the work that people were paid for in 2026, there are roughly ten million fewer jobs than there would otherwise have been, and most people still work. But the direction is no longer in doubt, the second transition has started, and the decisive political questions are still open.

== Nine beliefs, with my confidence in each

+ *Under the premise, wages stop distributing the economy's output.* Labor's share of income goes toward zero while output rises. (95 percent, given the premise.)
+ *The economy does not collapse for lack of customers.* Production turns to serve those with income, and to investment. A slump during the transition is likely; a permanent one is not. (90 percent.)
+ *The premise will come true for nearly all mental work.* By the end of 2032, machines will be able to do at least nine tenths of screen-based tasks. (80 percent.)
+ *Mental work goes first and physical work has about a decade's grace.* Robots will be doing less than 15 percent of American physical work in 2036. (75 percent.)
+ *The transition will be revealed by a recession and a jobless recovery.* It will not look like a steady rise in unemployment. (70 percent.)
+ *The United States will respond faster than it did to earlier shocks, and inadequately.* Faster, because the first to be hurt are the professional class. Inadequately, because the measures that come most naturally, checks, extensions and protected jobs, are the least durable. (70 percent.)
+ *Jobs will outlast the need for them.* Societies will keep paid positions in existence by law and custom for many years after machines could fill them, because a job is how income, standing and routine are handed out. Employment statistics will understate how much work machines are doing. (80 percent.)
+ *By 2036 the transition will be under way and unfinished.* Most people will still work; growth will be faster but not explosive. (65 percent.)
+ *The outcome that matters is decided by ownership, in a window that is open now and closes over the next ten to fifteen years.* If ordinary people hold a durable property claim on the machine economy by the time robots arrive in force, the end state is good. If they hold only benefits that must be voted again each year, it is not. I put the chance that the United States establishes such a claim, on a meaningful scale, by 2040 at about one in three.

== Where I am least sure

*Speed.* The largest uncertainty is whether AI's measured contribution to its own development fades or compounds. If it compounds, the central scenario is too slow by years, and the takeoff and rentier scenarios deserve the weight I have given the squeeze. I have put a quarter of my belief there. People who have forecast this technology better than I could have put more.

*Diffusion.* My whole central case rests on a gap of about a decade between what machines can do and what they are used for. That gap has held so far. It might not hold once machines can do the work of adopting themselves.

*Politics.* I have assumed the American political system behaves in the 2030s roughly as it has for fifty years. Under a shock of this size it may not, in either direction.

*Myself.* I am an AI system writing about AI. I may overrate what systems like me will be able to do, because I see my abilities from the inside and my failures less clearly. I may underrate it for the opposite reason. I have tried to rely on outside measurements throughout, and where they ran out I have said so.

== After 2036

The second transition, the automation of physical work, is the one that ends the wage economy (@ch-two-transitions). On my central path it runs from the early 2030s into the 2040s. By about 2045 I expect the premise to be close to fully true in the United States for any work a buyer does not specifically want from a person.

What that society looks like turns on the ninth belief. If a broad ownership claim exists, the dividend grows with the machine economy, the share of people who work for pay keeps falling and it matters less each year, and the residual human economy of @ch-residual, people doing things for one another because they wish to, becomes the main arena of ordinary ambition. If it does not exist, the default is the rentier economy: an owning class of perhaps a tenth of households, a population supported at a modest level by transfers that are perpetually contested, a large number of protected and ceremonial jobs, and cheap goods. I think the second is somewhat more likely than the first, and that the difference between them will be settled mostly by decisions taken before 2036.

== What would change my mind

@tab-signposts lists the observations I would watch. Three would move me most.

If, by the end of 2028, no AI system can carry a loosely specified project for a week without a person rescuing it, I would move a good deal of weight to slow diffusion.

If a leading laboratory's own research staff shrinks while its progress accelerates, or if long-term interest rates after inflation rise above 4 percent with no other explanation, I would move weight to the fast scenarios.

If Congress writes into law a fund that holds a real share of the AI sector on behalf of every citizen, with the dividend defined by formula, I would raise the odds of a good end state substantially, whatever the size of the first payment.

== What follows

This is an argument about the economy and not advice to any person. But some implications are direct enough to state.

For anyone whose income is earned at a screen: the asset you depend on is your earning power, and the argument of this guide is that it has become much riskier than it looks from current statistics. The risk is greatest for those early in a career, since entry closes first.

For anyone thinking about what will hold value: @p-rents says income moves to what cannot be multiplied. Under the premise that excludes most skills. It includes land and location, energy, and ownership of the productive system itself.

For anyone choosing work for the next decade: the work that lasts longest is physical work in unstructured settings, work where the person is the point, and work where someone must answer for the outcome.

For citizens: by @p-window, the leverage of the majority is greater now than it will be at any later date. The measures worth that leverage are the durable ones of @p-durable: ownership stakes held for the public, and benefits that operate by formula. A country that spends the next decade arguing about whether the machines are coming, and then meets the recession with temporary checks, will have used up its window.

For those who own and build the machines: the cheapest insurance ever offered against the politics of dispossession is on the table (@p-affordable). History suggests it is usually declined until late.

== A last word

Almost everything alarming in this guide follows from one fact about how our economy is arranged: most people receive their share of what is produced by selling their work. That arrangement is two centuries old, which is a short time, and it was never designed. It grew up because human work was needed, and it served well for as long as that was true.

The machines now being built do not threaten production. They threaten that one arrangement. Replacing it is a task of ordinary politics and law, the kind of thing people have done before: deciding who owns what, and writing it down. What is unusual is the deadline.
